{"kind":"report","text":"Decision on 6febb25a, successor of b35c28a4 (planner, 2026-10-21): F2 contract boundaries. Floor note: notes/2026-10-20-retained-journal-floor.md (main d92cd85e).\n\n1. Digest. The rule is equality of the same logical state, not equality before and after the floor entry. Moving accepted keys into the accepted-key table and copying request hashes into request rows must leave stateDigest(view.all()) of that state unchanged: all() returns the same accepted set and the same request set from the new tables (sections 6.4, 6.5, 6.8). The floor entry itself changes head, time, the floor, the kept set and the size-proof state, as any entry changes head and time. At every position, the scope and replay produce equal complete digests, before, at and after each floor. Drop no field to make two digests match. Section 6.4's sentence \"the state digest ... does not change at a floor entry\" means this, and a later planner head corrects its wording.\n\n2. Price. Section 5's fixed charge for a migrated accepted key is 256 bytes plus the UTF-8 byte length of its idempotency key. The key has no length bound of its own (packages/bytes/src/records.ts:126), so a flat price cannot bound the row; the intent's own bound bounds the key. The charge is added to logicalBytes once, in the floor entry's commit, and replay computes the same sum. No cap changes.\n\n3. Transitive reads. Yes. The kept set from state (section 4.1) includes the transitive closure of typed own references that live readers follow from each kept entry: application install creation origins (application-install-creation.ts:63-65, application-install.ts:124-126, :261) and the destination's prior mint operation and outcome (destination.ts:1243-1251), and every other typed own reference a reader of a kept entry follows. It never follows prev. The closure counts toward floor-kept, capped at 4,096; above the cap the floor entry is refused with limit, and the writer chooses a lower F. F2's report lists each reference kind followed, with its reader, and a witness reads a live install and a destination mint chain after a floor.\n\n4. Pending and results. Keep the sending entry of every request that can still receive a result that needs it:\n   - every row that pendingRequests returns, delivery-unavailable included (sqlite.ts:263; state.ts:244-246, :545);\n   - every create request with an applied result, because another incarnation's applied result can still arrive as a conflict, and runClause reads the origin entry (delivery.ts:319-330; handlers.ts:535-536).\n   Only an undelivered finding, or a result on a request that is not a create, ends the need (delivery.ts:316-330). Section 6.5's sentence that a completed request does not need its sending entry holds with this one exception, and a later planner head corrects it. F2's report states how many create-sending entries Room A's kept set holds at the F3 floor; if that number threatens the cap, the planner designs archived handling for late conflicts then. Do not design it now.\n\n5. Folded fields. Section 4.1(5) keeps an own position only when a reader follows it to read the entry. A position that a folded row holds only as an identity or a counter, such as account.seq and the capacity-request keyed seq of a completed send, stays as a number, and its entry is not kept. A reader that follows such a position after a floor gets the below-floor answer of section 6.1, and a witness shows it. F2's report lists all ten folded kinds with kept or not kept and the reader of each. Foreign positions and arbitrary numbers are never own references.\n\nNo cap, bound, authority or production enablement changes. A later planner head corrects sections 4.1, 5, 6.4 and 6.5 to these decisions.","body":{"to":"builder"}}